  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "Cast shadows are highly salient cues for the perception of spatial layout and object motion in depth, especially in dynamic scenes.",
    "The visual system relies on strong a priori constraints, such as a stationary light source, stationary background, and stationary viewpoint, to disambiguate ambiguous shadow motion.",
    "These constraints enable reliable inferences about object motion in 3D, even overriding other depth cues like generic viewpoint and size constancy.",
    "In contrast, cast shadows provide weak or no information for the perception of surface shape.",
    "Correlated motion between an object and its shadow is a crucial cue for shadow labeling and linking objects to their shadows in dynamic scenes, making these percepts robust to luminance violations.",
    "The computational complexity of linking objects to shadows based on shape in static scenes suggests the visual system prioritizes simpler, motion-based cues."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "What criteria do human observers use to categorize a patch in the image as a shadow?",
    "How good are we at discriminating attached from cast shadows?",
    "What mechanisms led to the selection of particular constraints used by our visual system, and what is the contribution of exposition to natural scenes?",
    "Are attached shadows processed in the occipito-temporal stream (shape) and cast shadows in the occipito-parietal stream (location)?",
    "Do special neural mechanisms exist for measuring the correlated motion between objects and their cast shadow?",
    "How can a visual system compute spatial layout from cast shadow information (simple heuristic vs. cooperative resolution)?",
    "How can 'delocalized' information from cast shadows be integrated with more local forms of depth information such as stereopsis?"
  ],
